1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,080
One in six people lives with a disability.

2
00:00:03,080 --> 00:00:04,331
To a screen reader,

3
00:00:04,331 --> 00:00:06,195
this online shop sounds like:

4
00:00:06,195 --> 00:00:07,176
image.

5
00:00:07,176 --> 00:00:08,200
Link.

6
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:09,181
Image.

7
00:00:09,181 --> 00:00:11,656
Forty-seven accessibility failures.

8
00:00:11,656 --> 00:00:12,715
And in Europe,

9
00:00:12,715 --> 00:00:14,776
since June 2025,

10
00:00:14,776 --> 00:00:16,520
that's a legal problem.

11
00:00:17,133 --> 00:00:20,610
Jangkau is an agent that doesn't just audit the page.

12
00:00:20,610 --> 00:00:21,912
It fixes it.

13
00:00:21,912 --> 00:00:24,421
Every edit is re-checked in a real browser,

14
00:00:24,421 --> 00:00:25,842
and if it breaks anything,

15
00:00:25,842 --> 00:00:27,906
it's rolled back and tried again.

16
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:30,583
We traced every run with neatlogs,

17
00:00:30,583 --> 00:00:34,999
then set up a detection that flags each rule the agent failed to fix.

18
00:00:34,999 --> 00:00:36,465
Here's what it caught.

19
00:00:37,067 --> 00:00:38,326
Incident one.

20
00:00:38,326 --> 00:00:41,846
Our first verifier rejected correct label fixes.

21
00:00:41,846 --> 00:00:43,368
The span told us why:

22
00:00:43,368 --> 00:00:46,632
it counted rules still waiting in the queue as breakage.

23
00:00:46,632 --> 00:00:47,862
We fixed the check,

24
00:00:47,862 --> 00:00:50,950
and the shop went from forty-seven failures to zero.

25
00:00:51,567 --> 00:00:52,647
Incident two,

26
00:00:52,647 --> 00:00:54,588
on a real Bootstrap template.

27
00:00:54,588 --> 00:00:56,658
Contrast fixes kept failing.

28
00:00:56,658 --> 00:01:00,148
The traces showed a pixel diff punishing correct recolors,

29
00:01:00,148 --> 00:01:03,604
and then a hidden opacity of zero point six five.

30
00:01:03,604 --> 00:01:04,791
Edge-map diffs,

31
00:01:04,791 --> 00:01:05,935
measured feedback,

32
00:01:05,935 --> 00:01:10,218
and escalation to a stronger model took it from thirty-three to zero.

33
00:01:10,833 --> 00:01:11,977
Incident three,

34
00:01:11,977 --> 00:01:15,732
on the W3C's own inaccessible demo page.

35
00:01:15,732 --> 00:01:20,937
The Execution Failed detection caught one bad edit throwing away nineteen good ones.

36
00:01:20,937 --> 00:01:22,673
Now valid edits are kept:

37
00:01:22,673 --> 00:01:25,057
sixty-seven failures down to four.

38
00:01:25,667 --> 00:01:27,366
It also runs in CI,

39
00:01:27,366 --> 00:01:28,987
as a GitHub Action.

40
00:01:28,987 --> 00:01:31,006
Every failure lands in code scanning,

41
00:01:31,006 --> 00:01:34,027
mapped to WCAG and to the European standard,

42
00:01:34,027 --> 00:01:36,902
EN 301 549.

43
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:40,884
Entire recorded the coding sessions behind these fixes,

44
00:01:40,884 --> 00:01:42,905
as checkpoints on our commits.

45
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:46,196
And cfo.ai modeled the business:

46
00:01:46,196 --> 00:01:47,767
about six cents per page,

47
00:01:47,767 --> 00:01:49,892
an eighty-three percent gross margin,

48
00:01:49,892 --> 00:01:52,831
and a downside case that set our fair-use rule.

49
00:01:53,433 --> 00:01:54,585
Jangkau.

50
00:01:54,585 --> 00:01:56,606
So the web reaches everyone.
